{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789788628,"nonce":"tgVQFeUGiiAmPHfCgGinTosnb1c=","title":"A widened shift or modulo stops the program instead of running","message":"The widener admits every BinOp on a widened operand, but the SSA walker executes only Add, Sub, Mul, SDiv, UDiv, And, Or and Xor lane by lane; a widened `a[i] = a[i] \u003e\u003e k` reaches it as VecBinOp SShr and the run ends with \"Error: unsupported vector op SShr\" (reproducer: a NativeArray\u003cInt\u003e loop halving each element with `i = 1 + i` as the step, --mode hybrid). Cranelift cannot lower the form either: its vector shifts take a scalar amount and it has no vector integer division, so those functions would be refused wholesale. LLVM takes anything.\n\nFix: the widener refuses an operation without a lane form in every consumer (Decline::NoLaneForm). Add/Sub/Mul on any lanes, SDiv/UDiv on float lanes, And/Or/Xor on integer lanes; shifts and modulo never, integer division never. Wrapping arithmetic is safe on narrow lanes because it is congruent modulo the register width; the excluded operations are not.","files":null}]}